\f1\fs22 Piotr Dunhilton was enjoying his morning routine\'97black coffee, market reports, and the systematic crushing of hope\'97when his secure line rang with the particular tone reserved for catastrophes.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Stockwell from Fridge Force, sir." The voice had the hollow quality of a man whose worldview had been refrigerated and found wanting. "We have a situation."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"If this is about the quarterly cooling metrics\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Seventeen mansions were hit last night. All stasis units compromised. Gyges Discs stolen."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton's coffee mug stopped halfway to his lips. "Seventeen?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Seventeen, sir. The perpetrators left notes."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Notes?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"'The Dawkins Provides,' sir. With a postscript about evolutionary superiority and a crude drawing of what appears to be a pelvis. Possibly Elvis. Hard to tell."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton set down his mug with the care of someone handling explosives. "How did they bypass our security? Those systems are foolproof!"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"That's the thing, sir." Stockwell's voice cracked. "They used sonic weapons."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Sonic weapons? What kind of\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Elvis music, sir. Played at volumes that technically qualify as assault. Our systems... well..." A pause that cost careers. "They're programmed to respond to conventional threats. Breaking glass, thermal signatures, unauthorized heartbeats. Nobody thought to include a protocol for 'Jailhouse Rock' in B-flat at 140 decibels."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"You're telling me our entire security infrastructure was defeated by rockabilly?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"The technical term is 'aggressive nostalgic resonance,' sir. Apparently, when you play 'All Shook Up' loud enough, it creates standing waves that make our sensors think they're experiencing an earthquake. The AI gets confused and starts opening things to prevent structural damage."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton's eye twitched. "Opening things."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Yes, sir. Doors, windows, wine cellars. Mansion 7 actually offered them coffee. The smart home system achieved what our techs are calling 'musical Stockholm syndrome.'"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Get Hardman. Now."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Ten minutes later, Gerry Hardman stood in Dunhilton's office, somehow managing to look both attentive and bored.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"The Elvi stole seventeen Gyges Discs," Dunhilton said without preamble.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Seventeen?" Gerry's eyebrows performed a small ballet of surprise. "That's..."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Enough to rewrite reality while juggling. Yes. I'm aware." Dunhilton pulled up security footage on his wall screen. White Lincolns swarmed up mountain roads like automotive locusts. "They hit the empty mansions. The ones we thought were secure because nobody would be stupid enough to rob houses that empty."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Apparently, the Elvi don't subscribe to conventional wisdom."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"They don't subscribe to conventional anything!" Dunhilton jabbed at the screen. "Look at this! They're not even trying to hide! Blue lights, sirens, a full convoy! It's like being robbed by a parade!"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry studied the footage. "The logistics are actually impressive. Seventeen targets, synchronized extraction, no casualties\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"They stole a truffle!"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"I'm sorry?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Mansion 4 had a stasis unit dedicated to a single white truffle. Worth eight thousand credits. They took that too!" Dunhilton's face achieved new shades of purple. "Left a note saying 'The Dawkins appreciates your contribution to evolutionary cuisine.'"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Creative."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"This isn't funny, Hardman! If the Elvi have Gyges Discs..." Dunhilton trailed off, his imagination failing at the scope of potential disasters.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"They could make their own stasis units," Gerry suggested helpfully. "Corner the market on time-stopped sequins."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Or they could destabilize reality itself! Do you know what happens when you put multiple discs in proximity without proper containment?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Theoretically\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Rotherhithe! The entire district exists in seventeen different timelines now! You can't get pizza delivered because the drivers keep arriving before they've left!"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry nodded thoughtfully. "What do you want to do?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Eloise Shakti." Dunhilton spat the name like expired milk. "This has her fingerprints all over it. Using the Elvi as pawns, acquiring discs for her research..."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Project Kebab," Gerry murmured.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Project Kebab. Even the name is insulting." Dunhilton pulled up a map of Sublime Point. "She thinks she's untouchable in that compound. Private army, advanced weapons, the moral high ground of a mountain."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Are we declaring war?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"War?" Dunhilton smiled, and somewhere a small animal died of fright. "War is what happens between equals. This is pest control."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"The Elvi won't\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"The Elvi are done. Finished. The TMinus virus should have handled them, but they found a way around it. Fine. We'll be more direct." He turned to Gerry. "Mobilize everything. I want Sublime Point surrounded by Thursday. If Eloise wants to play with reality, she can do it from a detention cell."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"And the Elvi?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"What about them?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"They have seventeen Gyges Discs. Possibly more. That's enough to\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"To what? Turn the Ghetto into a temporal paradise? Make Elvis evolve?" Dunhilton laughed. "They're clones with delusions of grandeur. Once we have Eloise, they'll hand over the discs and go back to their karaoke bars where they belong."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry made a note. "Should I inform PAPER?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"PAPER couldn't find its own acronym with a dictionary. No, we handle this ourselves." Dunhilton moved to his window, looking out over a city that didn't know its reality was about to be contested. "Fridge Force will want their discs back. Tell them they'll get them. Eventually. After we've made some examples."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Examples?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"The Airborne mansions were hit because they were empty. Vulnerable. Soft targets." His reflection smiled in the glass. "Sublime Point is about to learn what happens when you make the CEO of Ulyssia feel vulnerable."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"I'll begin preparations."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Oh, and Hardman?" Dunhilton didn't turn. "Find out which Elvi led the heist. I want names, numbers, favourite jumpsuits. Everything."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Already on it. Security footage shows someone called Major Memphis coordinating the operation."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Major Memphis." Dunhilton tasted the name. "Ridiculous. Like being robbed by a tribute act."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Technically, sir, they're all tribute acts."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Not for much longer."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As Gerry left, Dunhilton remained at the window. Somewhere out there, Elvi were probably celebrating their heist, unaware they'd just signed their own extinction notice.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And somewhere else, seventeen Gyges Discs sat in guitar cases, waiting to reshape reality.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The only question was who would reshape it first.\

